WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.273
- This is our trash can.

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:03.284
- [Mother] There's nothing in there!

00:00:03.284 --> 00:00:05.867
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.926 --> 00:00:06.759
- Hello.

00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.810
- I just landed in Sacramento

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:11.383
and I'm here to visit a zero-waste family,

00:00:11.450 --> 00:00:13.920
which I've been following on
Instagram for about a year.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.770
And I'm really excited to meet them,

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:18.495
'cause I want to see how
a family lives zero waste.

00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:19.697
- Hi.
- Hi.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.253
- This is how much trash that
our family made in six months.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.160
We're a family of four and we try to live

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.010
as low waste and plant-based as possible.

00:00:30.010 --> 00:00:33.080
Our family is able to create less waste

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.430
because we shop at the farmer's market,

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:38.393
we shop at our bulk store,
and we eat plant-based.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:40.060
- This is not all of our trash,

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.470
this is just the trash that we've kept

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.500
that is gonna go to the landfill.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.610
We also recycle and compost.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.733
This is our compost,
it's our weekly pickup.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:53.030
This is our paper recycling.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:57.420
This is usually full of
my stuff, of aluminum.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:59.380
They fed me an amazing lunch and then we

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.280
headed to the car to do some shopping.

00:01:01.289 --> 00:01:02.136
(gasps)

00:01:02.136 --> 00:01:04.073
Look at this, this is beautiful.

00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:07.127
I cannot believe this bulk section.

00:01:07.127 --> 00:01:07.960
- Isn't it great?
- It's amazing.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.613
- [Mother] Yeah, we're really lucky.

00:01:13.074 --> 00:01:13.907
Yes, exactly.

00:01:13.907 --> 00:01:16.310
That way you only have
to pay for what's inside,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:17.330
not the weight of the jar.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.150
I'm gonna get some of this
extra virgin olive oil

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.523
and I can just fill it up
out of here with no plastic.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.200
They have the mason jars
that are already tared here

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:29.050
so you can just purchase one if you forget

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:31.473
your own, it has the tare weight on it.

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.040
- You're such a good helper.

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:35.793
I got to get my bulk on while I'm here.

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:38.060
- The Co-Op has brought on bulk tofu

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:40.600
which is amazing because
it's local and organic

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.763
and there's no plastic packaging.

00:01:42.910 --> 00:01:44.480
- [Auri] What did your
kitchen use to look like

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.153
before you went zero-waste?

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:49.710
- It was stuffed to the brim with plastic,

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.500
like it was really hard
to see in the fridge even,

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.310
because we would just, would
go to Costco or to Trader Joe's

00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:00.390
and stuff and just get foods
that were wrapped in plastic

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.653
because I mean that's just how
things come, unfortunately.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:05.133
Here's my pantry.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.260
In here I've got all my spices.

00:02:07.260 --> 00:02:10.950
So I store most of our stuff in mason jars

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:12.510
just because it's so convenient.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.670
- [Auri] I honestly cannot
believe your pantry,

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:15.599
I'm so jealous.

00:02:15.599 --> 00:02:16.432
It's like, beautiful.

00:02:16.432 --> 00:02:18.097
Look at it, it's amazing.

00:02:18.097 --> 00:02:18.980
(laughs)

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.710
- It's really nice to be able
to see everything in there.

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:24.790
I came to a plant-based diet after I was

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:27.350
looking for a way to
heal my body holistically

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:29.143
from rheumatoid arthritis.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.610
And, after I started eating that way

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.230
I started to feel really good and

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.370
so I got my family to eat that way too.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:38.180
And it felt amazing and it also ties in

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:40.410
a lot with zero-waste and sustainability.

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:42.580
We got into zero-waste by me coming

00:02:42.580 --> 00:02:44.700
across Bea Johnson in an article,

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.010
and I was really inspired by the way that

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.553
she was able to live
and create less waste.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:52.170
And so we went really in at first

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:54.430
of trying to get everything zero-waste

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.230
and it just didn't work that way for us.

00:02:56.230 --> 00:02:57.960
And so we scaled back and just did

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.203
more manageable steps one at a time.

00:03:00.460 --> 00:03:02.493
And built our habits that way.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:04.160
- Eating zero-waste and plant-based

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.410
for the family has been very beneficial.

00:03:06.410 --> 00:03:09.310
We're finding ourselves
getting sick less often.

00:03:09.310 --> 00:03:11.683
We're eating as a family as a whole:

00:03:11.830 --> 00:03:15.910
all pitching in, cooking together,
going to farmer's market.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:17.370
The kids are growing it,

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.703
they're seeing it from seed to plate.

00:03:19.703 --> 00:03:22.700
- I think that overall we
have ended up saving money

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:25.400
as far as not any of
our food going to waste.

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.163
We use all the food that we buy.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:30.590
And as far as not getting
any disposable products,

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:33.100
it's a lot less expensive that way

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:35.138
because all our stuff is being reused.

00:03:35.138 --> 00:03:38.012
- The other kids are
surprised at my lunches

00:03:38.012 --> 00:03:41.260
because I don't think they have ever,

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.120
really seen something like that.

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.740
So it's a new thing for them.

00:03:44.740 --> 00:03:46.641
- I'm gonna shuck some corn with the kids!

00:03:46.641 --> 00:03:48.286
Do you guys like helping to cook?

00:03:48.286 --> 00:03:50.650
- Yeah I like helping to cook because

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:52.473
I want to learn to be a cook too.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.690
- We're having a delicious
dinner, thank you so much!

00:03:55.690 --> 00:03:56.840
- Thank you for coming.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:00.533
- We got toothpaste, homemade toothpaste.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:03.950
- I make our own
toothpaste with baking soda

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.514
and coconut oil and
peppermint essential oil.

00:04:06.514 --> 00:04:07.347
- [Kid] Oh!

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:08.430
- Hey, Unsolved is on a new channel!

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.263
And now your part.

00:04:09.263 --> 00:04:10.096
- Subscribe here.
- Subscribe here.

00:04:10.096 --> 00:04:11.183
- That was my part.

